@place ferry_stern
  travel 0
  travelFrom ferry_deck=3 ferry_lounge=2 ferry_bridge=3
  onArrive stern_first
  object jessica sprite=jessica x=20 y=96 h=66 label="Girl with the caskets" scene=ferry_jessica when day=1 and time<10:40 and not met:jessica
  object jessica_m sprite=jessica x=20 y=96 h=66 label="Jessica" scene=ferry_jessica when day=1 and time<10:40 and met:jessica
  object ashton sprite=ashton x=36 y=94 h=70 label="Boy with a rose" scene=ferry_ashton when day=1 and time<10:40 and not met:ashton
  object ashton_m sprite=ashton x=36 y=94 h=70 label="Ashton" scene=ferry_ashton when day=1 and time<10:40 and met:ashton
  object victoria sprite=victoria x=56 y=88 h=60 label="Girl with a parasol" scene=ferry_victoria when day=1 and time<10:40 and not met:victoria
  object victoria_m sprite=victoria x=56 y=88 h=60 label="Victoria" scene=ferry_victoria when day=1 and time<10:40 and met:victoria
  object sunny sprite=sunny x=80 y=100 h=68 label="Girl on the rail" scene=ferry_sunny when day=1 and time<10:40 and not met:sunny
  object sunny_m sprite=sunny x=80 y=100 h=68 label="Sunny" scene=ferry_sunny when day=1 and time<10:40 and met:sunny
  object freddie sprite=freddie x=104 y=98 h=56 label="Girl with goggles on her head" scene=ferry_freddie when day=1 and time<10:40 and not met:freddie
  object freddie_m sprite=freddie x=104 y=98 h=56 label="Freddie" scene=ferry_freddie when day=1 and time<10:40 and met:freddie
  object marit sprite=marit x=92 y=94 h=64 label="Girl on a bollard" scene=ferry_marit when day=1 and time<10:40 and not met:marit
  object marit_m sprite=marit x=92 y=94 h=64 label="Marit" scene=ferry_marit when day=1 and time<10:40 and met:marit
  action wake "Watch the wake and the gulls" scene=stern_wake when day=1 and time<10:40
  action cage "Look through the luggage cage" scene=stern_cage when day=1 and time<10:40

@place ferry_bridge
  travel 0
  travelFrom ferry_deck=2 ferry_lounge=2 ferry_stern=3
  onArrive bridge_first
  object abel sprite=abel x=62 y=96 h=74 label="The captain" scene=ferry_abel when day=1 and time<10:40 and not met:abel
  object abel_m sprite=abel x=62 y=96 h=74 label="Captain Stroud" scene=ferry_abel when day=1 and time<10:40 and met:abel
  object rana sprite=rana x=24 y=100 h=48 label="Scowling girl" scene=ferry_rana when day=1 and time<10:40 and not met:rana
  object rana_m sprite=rana x=24 y=100 h=48 label="Rana" scene=ferry_rana when day=1 and time<10:40 and met:rana
  object teobalt sprite=teobalt x=84 y=94 h=62 label="Boy with a clipboard" scene=ferry_teobalt when day=1 and time<10:40 and not met:teobalt
  object teobalt_m sprite=teobalt x=84 y=94 h=62 label="Teobalt" scene=ferry_teobalt when day=1 and time<10:40 and met:teobalt
  action list "Read the passenger list" scene=bridge_list when day=1 and time<10:40
  action plate "Read the brass plate by the door" scene=bridge_plate when day=1 and time<10:40
  action chart "Look at the chart" scene=bridge_chart when day=1 and time<10:40
  action binoculars "Borrow the binoculars" scene=bridge_binoculars when day=1 and time<10:40
  action overlook "Look down on the ship" scene=bridge_overlook when day=1 and time<10:40

# ───────────────────────────── HECK ─────────────────────────────

@scene stern_first once bg=ferry_stern
  You follow the side deck aft past the funnel, which breathes warmth over your shoulder like a large, sleeping animal. And then the noise falls away.
  The open after deck: a long white wake running back to an empty horizon, gulls riding the air above the flagstaff, a wire luggage cage stacked to the top with trunks. One bench stands dry under a blue-and-white striped awning, a lifebuoy hung on the rail beside it. The rest of the planking shines with spray.
  It is quieter here than forward. The mainland is gone.
  Half a dozen people are scattered across the deck, each in their own small piece of calm, and none of them is quarrelling with anybody. A girl is reciting to the gulls. A boy is standing very nobly at the rail holding a rose. Beside the cage, a very still girl in a ruffled dress sits alone under a parasol.
  ~ So this is where people go who want to meet each other.
  @time 3

@scene stern_wake
  The wake is a long white seam, stitched behind the ship and closing as it goes. Gulls ride the air above it with their wings fixed, barely moving. They watch the water like accountants.
  You stand at the rail with the wind behind you and say nothing to anyone. It is, you realise, the longest you have been quiet since you got on the boat.
  ~ Nobody is looking at me. Nobody is waiting for me to do anything.
  It feels, for a moment, like a gift.
  @if not found_lenscap
    Something small and round has rolled into the corner by the scuppers and is rattling there with every swell. A black lens cap, with a name scratched inside it in careful capitals: LUND P.
    ~ Somebody on this ship is going to be very unhappy about this.
    @item "Pippa's lens cap"
    @flag found_lenscap
  @time 8

@scene stern_cage
  The luggage cage is a wire pen stacked to the top with trunks, duffels, and one inexplicable tuba. Every item has a paper label tied to it, handwritten.
  You read some. HALVORSEN B. — FRAGILE (a very large hard case that you suspect holds nothing breakable at all). WHISPER N. — OPEN ME AND DIE (a battered duffel, with a smaller, tidier label beneath that says in another hand: *please don't*). CAPULET V. — HANDLE WITH GRACE. LUND P. — CAMERA, DON'T. THANA O. — one small bag, tied twice.
  At the bottom of the pile, sagging, you find your own duffel. The label says, in Aunt Dee's handwriting: ROWAN — CALL HER WHEN YOU LAND.
  ~ I will. I will call her the minute I know what to say.
  @flag found_duffel
  @time 5

@scene ferry_jessica
  @if not met:jessica
    Beside a crewman's trolley stacked with three leather caskets, a beautiful girl sits with one ringed hand resting on the smallest.
    Long golden curls under a small gold coronet set with a red stone and a blue one, blue eyes, a small beauty mark under the left.
    A fine gold chain at her throat with a small swallow charm on it, chosen fresh this morning and worn as if it were nothing. Rings on three fingers, each of them a different stone.
    A mild, patient look on her face, the look of someone who has been approached by a great many people with a great many lines.
    She has, you notice, a very particular way of sitting: perfectly upright, perfectly still, her ankles crossed, as though a portrait were being painted and the artist had warned her not to blink.
    She is not looking at you. She has, however, noticed you crossing the deck.
    @choice
      > "Hello. I'm Rowan. I wanted to say it properly, because nobody else seems to have crossed the deck." @hint Plain
        [jessica?] "Hello."
        She looks up. A small, real smile, like a candle catching.
        [jessica?] "That's the first time this morning anyone has crossed the deck to say that and nothing else."
        [jessica?] "Jessica Montclair. I would shake your hand, but I do not take my hand off these."
        A tilt of the head at the caskets.
        @meet jessica
        @flag jessica_plain
      > "Those caskets look like they hold either jewels or a very elegant corpse." @hint Line
        [jessica?] "How lovely."
        It is a very warm, very final phrase.
        [jessica?] "Those are my jewels. Not the corpses you were about to suggest."
        [jessica?] "Jessica Montclair."
        @meet jessica
        @aff jessica +1 | Made a joke
      > "Forgive me, but the sea has never looked less beautiful than it does next to you." @hint Cheesy
        [jessica?] "Ah."
        A gracious, faintly sorrowful lift of the brow.
        [jessica?] "I have been addressed that way four times this morning. By the same boy. You are, I think, an imitation, and I am not sure how I feel about that."
        [jessica?] "Jessica Montclair."
        @meet jessica
        @aff jessica -2 | Used a line
    @add ferry_talked +1
    @note jessica | Gracious and formal. Travels with three caskets of jewellery.
    @if jessica_plain
      [jessica] "Where are you going to be living?"
      [rowan] "The Commons. Bungalow seven."
      [jessica] "Spectral Tower. I have a room on the second floor, apparently. With a view of the volcano."
      A small, wry shrug.
      [jessica] "Nobody has told me yet whether I am meant to find that pleasant."
      [rowan] "Do you?"
      [jessica] "I find it very large. I find that I may like it, in time." She looks at the island for a moment. "It is nice to be asked."
      @note jessica | Staying in the Spectral Tower, second floor, with a view of the volcano.
    @else
      [jessica] "You must forgive me. I am not accustomed to being spoken to without an introduction. It is an old habit."
      [rowan] "I'm sorry."
      [jessica] "Do not be. It is a good habit to be broken of." She says it very lightly. "It is just that it has been a long morning of lines."
    @time 8
  @else
    @if not jessica_game_after_docks
      [jessica] "Do sit."
      @choice
        > "May I keep you company for a while? I'm not much of a talker, but I'd like to hear about your crossing."
          For a few minutes the two of you watch the wake. She asks you real questions, and waits for the answers. It is an odd and pleasant feeling, like being held in warm light.
          [jessica] "I don't chase, Rowan. It isn't pride. It's that I should like to know someone crossed the room on purpose."
          @aff jessica +2 | Stayed to talk
          @time 15
        > "Would you like a game after all? I'll understand if the answer is still no." @hint She declined earlier
          [jessica] "On a boat? With my caskets in the way?" A gracious headshake. "After we dock. If you remember to ask me then."
          @flag jessica_game_after_docks
        > "May I ask you a few things, if you've the patience?" @if not chat_jessica_done
          @scene chat_jessica
        > Excuse yourself.
          [jessica] "Of course. Go and cross some other deck."
    @else
      [jessica] "Rowan."
      @time 3
  @if met:jessica and not chat_jessica_offered
    @flag chat_jessica_offered
    @scene chat_jessica

@scene ferry_ashton
  @if not met:ashton
    A tall, slim boy with violet hair swept up into one smooth dome stands very nobly at the rail, holding a single rose by the stem. His other hand is turning a thorn between finger and thumb.
    He is trying one line after another. Not one of them is working.
    [ashton?] "A rose for the lady, and my condolences to every man she met before me."
    The lady in question, thirty feet away, is looking at the sea.
    [ashton?] "Ah. An audience."
    He swings round, seeing you for the first time, with the dazzling, wounded ease of a man who has not noticed he is wounded.
    [ashton?] "Ashton Beaumont-Lyle. You'll know the name. If you don't, nobody does."
    @meet ashton
    @add ferry_talked +1
    @note ashton | Wealthy. Gives roses to every woman and talks like a novel.
    [ashton] "Vance, isn't it. The Wildcard. I can add up, old thing: a rain jacket, no collar, and a name in ink on the list at the wheelhouse door."
    [rowan] "That's — fair. I'm Rowan, and apparently I'm easy to work out."
    [ashton] "Not at all. You have a very quiet face. It's the jacket that gives you away."
    He sets a thumbnail against the stem of the rose and, with three exact movements, removes a thorn you could not see.
    [ashton] "I do this for every rose. It is a courtesy. One should not give a lady a thing that could hurt her without asking."
    [rowan] "That's — actually quite nice."
    [ashton] "Don't say it as if it were a surprise, old thing. I am a *very* nice person. It is simply a long way down."
  @else
    [ashton] "Vance! Rescue me."
  @choice
    > "Is that working? From where I'm standing, she hasn't looked round in about ten minutes." @hint Gestures at Jessica
      [ashton] "No." Cheerfully. "But she hasn't asked me to stop, and I have a theory that persistence is a kind of conversation."
      [ashton] "I lost at the lounge table half an hour ago, so I'm quite used to it."
      @flag ashton_lost_table
      @aff ashton +1 | Made him laugh
    > "You mentioned the lounge table. What happened to you down there?" @if ashton_lost_table or table_resolved
      [ashton] "Gideon." A shrug of the shoulders. "He did nothing. For nine turns he did nothing. I attacked, he sat there, I attacked more, and when I'd finished hanging myself, he took my head off."
      [ashton] "Elegant, really. I have never been so thoroughly beaten by someone doing so little."
      @flag saw_counter
      @aff ashton +1 | Shared the story
    > "Have you heard what happened at the table since? Let me tell you how it ended." @if table_resolved
      @if ferry_table_won
        [ashton] "Beaten! By YOU! Do you know what that is worth?" He is delighted. "He carried it very well. Said nothing. Looked ill. Bravo."
      @elif ferry_inya_won
        [ashton] "Beaten by a child with bunnies! I wish I could have seen his face."
      @elif ferry_table_lost
        [ashton] "Oh, my dear boy. Four cases. Up that hill. I'd say bring a hat."
      @elif ferry_table_settled
        [ashton] "You — made him play for nothing. I want to hear about that for an hour."
      @elif ferry_table_denied
        [ashton] "He wouldn't play her at all? How perfectly like him. Poor child."
      @else
        [ashton] "The child lost. She attacked first. I could have told her."
      @aff ashton +1 | Brought news
    > "Would you fancy a casual game, Ashton? No stake, just something to do while the lady makes up her mind." @if not flag_ashton_game
      [ashton] "For no stake. Lazily. Is she watching?" He glances at Jessica, who is not. "She is. Very well."
      @duel ferry_ashton deck=ashton name="Ashton Beaumont-Lyle"
        @win
          [ashton] "Poisoned the field and still lost. How instructive. Naturally." A beat. "…What was the question?"
          @flag beat_ashton
          @aff ashton +2 | Good sport
        @lose
          [ashton] "Do tell the lady, if she asks, that I won gracefully."
          @aff ashton +2 | Won gracefully
      @scene post_ashton
      @flag flag_ashton_game
      @time 25
    > "Let's talk for a bit. I'd like to know what you make of all this." @if not chat_ashton_done
      @scene chat_ashton
    > Go and speak to the girl with the caskets.
      [ashton] "Do. Do. And tell her I said hello. Or that I said nothing. Or don't."
  @time 6
  @if met:ashton and not chat_ashton_offered and not flag_ashton_game
    @flag chat_ashton_offered
    @scene chat_ashton

@scene ferry_victoria
  @if not met:victoria
    Under the one dry striped awning, on the one dry bench, a girl in a ruffled dress sits with a lace parasol open over her head, although she is already under an awning. Long pale-gold ringlets tied with ribbons. Wide sky-blue eyes that rest on things rather than people. She is watching the wake.
    She does not look round.
    @choice
      > "Excuse me. I'm Rowan. Is it all right if I sit with you for a moment? I won't talk much." @hint Plain
        [victoria?] "Yes." She turns her head, slowly. "I should like that. I have been here since Tuesday." A pause. "That is a joke. I am told that I do not make them. I was practising."
        [victoria?] "Victoria Capulet."
        @aff victoria +3 | Asked her plainly
        @flag victoria_plain
      > "Is that for the sun, or in case the sea tries anything?" @hint Joke
        The sea goes on being the sea. A gull crosses the sky. Nothing at all happens, and goes on happening, for what feels like a full minute.
        ~ Either she didn't hear me, or that was the longest mistake of my life.
        [victoria?] "I did not hear you, because I was looking at the wake." She turns her head. The sky-blue eyes arrive on you like a slow, polite weather. "I have finished now."
        [victoria?] "It is for the sun, and in case the sea tries anything. Both. I like to be prepared, and it is also pretty. Victoria Capulet."
        @aff victoria +1 | Waited for her answer
        @flag victoria_parasol
      > "Hey, you, in the lace! What's the story with the parasol? Does it come with the dress, or is it an upgrade?" @hint Loud
        Heads turn along the rail. The silence from the bench is very polite. And then very, very long.
        [victoria?] "I did not hear you, because I was looking at the wake. I have finished now." She turns her head. "You spoke rather quickly, and rather loudly, and I could not tell which part was the question."
        [victoria?] "I answer one thing at a time. If you would be so good as to ask again, more slowly, I should like to answer. Victoria Capulet."
        @aff victoria -1 | Hurried her
        @flag victoria_hurried
    @meet victoria
    @add ferry_talked +1
    @time 8
  @else
    [victoria] "Rowan." She looks up. It is a very still, very attentive look. "The sea has been exactly the same colour since we left. I find that restful."
    @choice
      > "Would you play a game with me at some point? I'd like to see how you play." @if not victoria_game_after_docks @hint After docks
        [victoria] "I do not play in a crowd. After we dock. At a table." A faint, almost-smile. "I should like it very much."
        @flag victoria_game_after_docks
      > "May I ask you a few things? One at a time." @if not chat_victoria_done
        @scene chat_victoria
      > Let her be.
        [victoria] "Thank you."
    @time 4
  @if met:victoria and not chat_victoria_offered
    @flag chat_victoria_offered
    @scene chat_victoria

@scene ferry_sunny
  @if not met:sunny
    A tall girl is sitting on the lower bar of the rail with her bare feet dangling over the wake. A towel is slung round her neck like a scarf. Her ponytail is wet, although it has not rained.
    She is, at the exact moment you notice her, doing something with a deck of cards that involves flipping half of it into the air and catching it behind her back.
    She catches it. Every card. She does not look at them.
    She sees you looking and grins with her whole face.
    @if ship_knows_wildcard
      [sunny?] "You're the wildcard! Nice shoulders for a bloke who took the last seat. That's a compliment, don't make it weird."
    @else
      [sunny?] "You're the one without the jacket! Nice shoulders. That's a compliment, don't make it weird."
    [rowan] "Thank you, I think. I'm Rowan. I wasn't expecting to be complimented before breakfast."
    @add ferry_talked +1
    [sunny?] "Sunny Aldane. Sports scholarship. I'm the lifeguard, as of — what — Monday. Everything on the island's going to fall in the water, and I'm the one who fishes it out. It's literally the job."
    @meet sunny
    @note sunny | Lifeguard on a sports scholarship. Says what she thinks the moment she thinks it.
    [sunny] "I like you. There, said it."
    She says it so simply, with so little fuss, that you have no idea where to put your hands.
    [sunny] "Your turn. Or we can race for it."
    @choice
      > "You like me? You've known me for about thirty seconds. I'm Rowan, by the way."
        [sunny] "Takes me thirty. That's practically a marathon for me."
        [rowan] "And if you stop liking me?"
        [sunny] "Then I'll tell you. Same speed." She shrugs, entirely unbothered. "It saves a lot of time."
        @aff sunny +3 | Liked directly
      > "Race you where? I'm Rowan, and I should warn you I'm not especially fast."
        [sunny] "Off the stern to the lifeboat and back! No — kidding, I'd get us both thrown off."
        She laughs, a big, easy sound.
        [sunny] "Cards. Come on. Loser does ten push-ups."
        @aff sunny +2 | Played along
        @jump sunny_game
      > Go red and say nothing.
        [sunny] "Ha! There it is."
        She looks at you with open, delighted interest.
        [sunny] "That's a good colour on you."
        @aff sunny +1 | Blushed
    @time 8
  @else
    @label sunny_game
    @if not flag_sunny_game
      [sunny] "Right. Ten push-ups for the loser. On the planks."
      @choice
        > "You're on. Ten push-ups for the loser, on the planks."
          @duel ferry_sunny deck=sunny name="Sunny Aldane"
            @win
              [sunny] "No dramas!" She is already face-down on the planks, counting aloud, loudly, with great goodwill. "Seven! Eight! Nine — I'm lying, that was six. TEN!" She bounces up. "Good game, Wildcard!"
              @aff sunny +3 | Took the loss well
            @lose
              [sunny] "Ha!" Triumphant. "Ten! Go on! Face down!"
              You do ten. By the seventh your arms are shaking. By the tenth you are lying on the planks next to a seagull with the sun on your back, and Sunny is lying next to you, laughing, and you realise you are laughing too.
              @aff sunny +4 | Did the push-ups
          @scene post_sunny
          @flag flag_sunny_game
          @time 25
        > "Let me ask you something first, before I hit the planks." @if not chat_sunny_done
          @scene chat_sunny
        > "Not on a wet deck, I'll slip. Find me on land and I'll take your challenge."
          [sunny] "Chicken." Affectionately. "Come find me on land."
    @else
      [sunny] "Hey, Wildcard! Still alive?"
      @time 5
      @if not chat_sunny_done
        @choice
          > "Have you got a minute? I've got questions."
            @scene chat_sunny
          > Wave and move on.
  @if met:sunny and not chat_sunny_offered and not flag_sunny_game
    @flag chat_sunny_offered
    @scene chat_sunny

@scene ferry_marit
  @if not met:marit
    A girl with two tall flame-shaped tufts of hair, each one tied up with an orange bow, is standing on a mooring bollard, reciting to the gulls.
    "…and in that hour, when the sea went grey as a forgotten promise —" She has long flame-orange hair that flares up into two tall, flame-shaped tufts, one on each side of her head, and a scarf the colour of embers wound twice round her throat. She is holding an invisible sword aloft. The gulls are paying attention in the way gulls do, which is to say, as a business matter.
    She sees you. The recital stops.
    [marit?!] "A **HUMAN!** An *AUDIENCE!* The gods are *generous* this morning! **BEHOLD!**"
    [marit?!] "Marit Solberg! Commentary track! Theatre kid **EXTRAORDINAIRE!** Flame-adjacent in every conceivable respect! And *you* — you are the **BROODING STRANGER** without a jacket!"
    @meet marit
    @add ferry_talked +1
    [rowan] "I'm Rowan Vance, and I'm not brooding. I'm just underdressed."
    [marit!] "**UNDERDRESSED!** Rowan Vance — brooding, underdressed and *brand new!* I have decided, here and now, upon this very bollard, that we are having an **ARC!**"
    @choice
      > "What kind of arc did you have in mind? I'd like to know what I'm signing up for."
        [marit!] "Oh, the **GOOD** kind! The kind where you start out terrified of something and end up with a *very* good coat!" She grins. "I'll let you know."
        @aff marit +2 | Played along
      > "How about a game, then? Narrate away." @hint She narrates
        [marit!] "A **GAME!** Yes. Yes yes **YES!** And I shall narrate every move, because I physically cannot *help* it!"
        @jump marit_game
      > "Is all this a hobby, or a calling? You're very good at it."
        [marit!] "A **CALLING!**" Then — quieter, an aside, almost ordinary: "Also a hobby. I'm not 'too much'. I'm the correct amount for a larger room."
        @aff marit +3 | Saw behind the act
    @time 8
  @else
    @label marit_game
    @if not flag_marit_game
      [marit!] "A game! I shall narrate. You will hate it. You will *love* it. **BEHOLD!**"
      @choice
        > "All right, Marit. Let's play. And I'd like to hear the narration."
          A small crowd gathers around the bollard in the time it takes to shuffle. Six people, then eight. The deckhand with the tea, two girls from the lounge, a boy with a clipboard. Your hands begin to shake the moment you pick up the deck.
          ~ Everyone's watching. Not me, exactly. The game. But close enough.
          [marit!] "From the ashes of my frankly terrible opening hand — she **RETURNS!** *(I had no idea that would work.)*"
          @duel ferry_marit deck=marit name="Marit Solberg"
            @win
              [marit!] "**ALAS!** *DEFEAT!* And what a defeat, ladies and gentlemen, what a — no. No, it's a good one." She drops, for exactly one sentence, into an ordinary voice. "Rowan Vance, you have a very quiet kind of thunder."
              @aff marit +3 | Good game
            @lose
              [marit!] "The **PHOENIX**, ladies and gentlemen! **ALWAYS** the Phoenix!" She takes a very small, very private bow, then drops, mid-flourish, into her ordinary voice: "That was lovely. Thank you for letting me."
              @aff marit +3 | Played with her
          @scene post_marit
          @flag flag_marit_game
          @time 25
        > "Let me ask you something first, and then we play." @if not chat_marit_done
          @scene chat_marit
        > "Maybe later. I'd like to see the rest of the ship first."
          [marit!] "Later! The **SEQUEL!** The sequel is *always* better!"
    @else
      [marit!] "Rowan! **PROTAGONIST!** Have you seen the island? It has a **MOUNTAIN!** I have already written a monologue!"
      @time 5
      @if not chat_marit_done
        @choice
          > "Do you have a minute between monologues? I've got questions."
            @scene chat_marit
          > Applaud politely and move on.
  @if met:marit and not chat_marit_offered and not flag_marit_game
    @flag chat_marit_offered
    @scene chat_marit

# ───────────────────────────── BRÜCKE ─────────────────────────────

@scene bridge_first once bg=ferry_bridge
  You take the iron stair two steps at a time. At the top is a narrow wing of dark deck outside the wheelhouse, with a rope rail, a brass bell on the wall, a lifebuoy, and a fire axe in a red frame beside a clipboard.
  Through the open door you see a bright room of brass and polished wood: a big spoked wheel, a bank of dials, a red rug, a chart table, and through the front windows the island, dead ahead and larger now, its grey cone trailing a thread of smoke. A man in a navy suit and a blue bowler hat stands at the wheel with his feet apart, talking without turning round.
  If you lean over the rail, the whole ship lies below you.
  @time 3

@scene bridge_overlook
  From the wing you can see who is where. A knot of people at the lounge stair. A girl on the stern rail with her feet over the wake. A small crowd round a bollard where somebody is waving an arm at the gulls. Nathan, a spiky dot, in motion on the fore deck, talking at a very tall dot.
  @if table_resolved
    The lounge door lets out a ripple of noise, and then another. Whatever was going to happen at the table has happened.
  @else
    A small dot with a deck box stands at the end of a table. The deck has gone quiet, in the way decks do before something happens.
  @time 4

@scene bridge_plate
  A small brass plate is screwed to the wheelhouse door, bright where the sleeves of generations have rubbed it:
  M/V SECOND CHANCE — EVERYBODY GETS ONE.
  ~ That's either the most reassuring or the most alarming thing I've read all week.
  @time 2

@scene bridge_chart
  The chart is pinned flat by a chipped mug that says WORLD'S OKAYEST CAPTAIN. It shows the island as a rough, blunt oval with a volcano at the centre, the pier on the south coast, a bay with the town on its western curve, and a dotted line round the north shore labelled, in a very old hand, DO NOT.
  Somebody has drawn a small boat on the south side, dead on the pier, and written: *tie up 10.40*.
  @time 3

@scene bridge_binoculars
  The binoculars hang from a hook inside the door. Nobody stops you from borrowing them.
  Through the lenses the island becomes sharp and brilliant: the black rim of the crater, the green flanks, a white tower on the south cliff that must be the lighthouse. And, clear as a sketch, a long curve of pale beach with a row of tiny roofs along it. Seven of them. You count.
  ~ Bungalow row. Seven roofs. Mine's the last one, probably. Late admission, last bungalow.
  @flag saw_commons_roofs
  @time 5

@scene bridge_list
  The passenger list hangs on a clipboard beside the wheelhouse door, typed. You read down it.
  Thirty-seven names in a tidy column, and then, at the bottom, a thirty-eighth, in ink, squeezed in at a slight angle as if added in a hurry:
  *Vance, R. — Wildcard.*
  ~ Everyone who reads this list knows who the Wildcard is.
  @flag read_passenger_list
  @time 3

@scene ferry_abel
  @if not met:abel
    The man at the wheel is barrel-chested, with a full white beard and a blue bowler hat gone soft with age. His face is the colour and texture of a good saddle.
    Through the open door the wheelhouse is warm and bright with brass. He has both hands on the wheel, with his feet planted apart, as if the whole ship were a very large, very slow horse that needed to be reminded who was riding it.
    [abel?] "Thirty-seven and — hold on. Thirty-eight! There you are. I counted thirty-seven."
    He does not turn round.
    [abel?] "Captain Abel Stroud. Welcome to the Second Chance."
    @meet abel
    @add ferry_talked +1
    @note abel | The ferry captain. Counts every student on and off.
    [abel] "If you are going to be sick, do it leeward. That's the side that isn't me."
    [rowan] "I'm not sick. I'm just — looking."
    [abel] "That's the best thing to do on a bridge. Most of them come up here to complain."
  @else
    [abel] "Thirty-eight. Still thirty-eight."
  @choice
    > "Captain, when do we dock? I'd like to know how long I've got to find my nerve."
      [abel] "Ten-forty! On the nose! Not a minute before, not a minute after, or I will eat my hat. Which is soft. I've considered it."
      @aff abel +1 | Asked about docking
    > "How long have you been captain of this ferry, if you don't mind my asking?"
      [abel] "Twenty-two years. I've carried champions, and I've carried trunks home without their owners. I know which I'd rather."
      [abel] "Hundred and forty-four out in September. I like a hundred and forty-four back in June. Don't spoil my sums."
      @aff abel +2 | Asked about his work
    > "What is the weather going to do today, Captain?"
      [abel] "Fair, then lumpy, then fair. Same as everyone's morning." He laughs at his own joke, a big, rolling laugh. "That's one of six. I'll do the next one in half an hour."
      @aff abel +1 | Laughed at the joke
    > "Do you play cards yourself, Captain, or do you only watch?"
      [abel] "Not at sea. One game a year, on land, with Race Boats, which is a novelty deck and loses." A wink in his voice. "Ask me in May."
      @flag abel_game_may
      @aff abel +1 | Asked about cards
    > "Is there anything I can carry ashore for you?" @if not has_snapper
      [abel] "Hm." A very long pause, and a very small cough into his beard. "There is a crate of snapper under the tarp by the door. For Mrs Fuentes — runs the kitchen at the Commons. You'll know her. You can't miss her. She will be the one with the spoon."
      [abel] "No message. Just — it's good snapper."
      @flag has_snapper
      @aff abel +3 | Offered to carry the snapper
    > Thank him and go.
      [abel] "Mind the step!"
  @time 5

@scene ferry_rana
  @if not met:rana
    On the top step of the iron stair a girl is sitting with her arms round her knees and her back turned to the rail, as far from anybody as it is possible to be on a ship.
    Blonde hair in two long braids that hang down in front of her shoulders, each one tied off with a large red bow. Black nail polish, chipped from biting. A plain grey collar studded with tiny silver studs.
    She is watching the planks.
    She knows you are there. You can tell from the set of her shoulders, which have gone, very slightly, a little straighter, in the manner of an animal that has heard a twig.
    She does not look up.
    [rana?] "No."
    [rowan] "I haven't — "
    [rana?] "No. Whatever it is. No."
    @choice
      > Sit down on the stair near her. @hint Quiet
        She makes a very small noise in her throat. It is not welcome, but it is not a wall, either.
        You sit. You say nothing.
        The stair is cold iron, with salt on it. Below you, the whole ship is in motion, a thousand little conversations, and up here, in this narrow corner, it is oddly peaceful.
        After a while she speaks, without looking at you.
        [rana?] "…You can read, right? There's a list. I'm on it. I'm on a different list, too, with a different collar."
        A very quick, very bitter glance.
        [rana?] "Don't."
        [rowan] "Don't what?"
        [rana?] "Whatever you were going to ask."
        After a while, grudgingly, to the planks:
        [rana?] "…Rana. Since you're not going away."
        @meet rana
        @note rana | Sits apart. Says she is on a different list with a different collar.
        @aff rana +2 | Didn't push
      > "I'm Rowan. I don't mean to bother you. What's your name?"
        [rana?] "Rana. That's all you get."
        It is under six words, nearly.
        [rana?] "Why."
        [rowan] "I just wanted to know who I'd be sharing the stairs with."
        [rana?] "…You're not sharing them. I was here first."
        @meet rana
        @note rana | Scowls. Keeps everyone at a distance.
        @aff rana +1 | Asked plainly
      > "Why are you sitting all the way out here by yourself?"
        Her whole body goes rigid. The scowl, which looked permanent, finds another notch.
        [rana?] "Why do you care."
        [rana?] "…Rana. Now go away."
        @meet rana
        @aff rana -2 | Pushed
        @flag rana_pressed
    @add ferry_talked +1
    @time 6
  @else
    @if rana_pressed
      [rana] "Whatever."
      @time 2
    @else
      [rana] "…Yeah?"
      @choice
        > "Do you play? I'm not asking for a game. I just don't know what anybody's running." @if not deck_known_rana @hint Tentative
          @scene answer_rana
        > "Would it be all right if I sat here a while? I won't talk." @if not chat_rana_done
          @scene chat_rana
        > "Good to see you again."
          [rana] "Mm."
        > Leave her.
          [rana] "Good."
      @time 4


@scene ferry_teobalt
  @if not met:teobalt
    A boy with dark-blue hair standing up in neat spikes, in a long red coat buttoned to the collar, is standing very straight at the wheelhouse door with a clipboard. He is looking slightly over the heads of the crowd below, as if counting them. There is a spare pen behind his ear.
    He is not so much waiting as supervising the idea of waiting. Every few seconds the pen leaves his ear, makes a very small mark on the clipboard, and returns to its place with a tiny click.
    [teobalt?] "Excuse me. Do you know in what order the luggage comes off? It is a question of logistics."
    [rowan] "I — no, I'm sorry. I came aboard late and I don't know anything about the unloading. I'm Rowan."
    [teobalt?] "No. Nobody does. I have asked the captain, and he says it comes off in the order it comes off."
    [teobalt?] "That is, I'm afraid, not an order."
    [teobalt?] "Teobalt Fullington. I have drawn up a list."
    @meet teobalt
    @add ferry_talked +1
    @note teobalt | Keeps lists and a clipboard. Worried about the unloading order.
    @choice
      > "Do you mind if I see your list? It sounds like it might save us all a lot of trouble."
        [teobalt] "Three things."
        A rustle, a page, a pen.
        [teobalt] "One: the unloading order, which I have taken the liberty of estimating. Two: who has my stapler, which is not relevant yet. Three is confidential until I have solved one and two."
        He tears out a clean copy and hands it over. It is colour-coded.
        [teobalt] "I used teal for the items I am worried about."
        [rowan] "There's a lot of teal."
        [teobalt] "I am a worrying person." A very small, very pleased pause. "It is a gift."
        @item "Teobalt's unloading list"
        @flag has_unloading_list
        @note teobalt | Has lost his stapler. Item three on his list is confidential.
        @aff teobalt +3 | Wanted the list
      > Hand him the stapler you found. @if found_stapler and not stapler_returned
        [teobalt] "That is — "
        He stops. He takes it in both hands, turns it over, finds his own stencil, and for a moment says nothing at all.
        [teobalt] "Item two. Solved."
        [teobalt] "I had it down as stolen. I had made a list of suspects."
        A very small, very bright pause.
        [teobalt] "You are not on it. You have never been on it."
        @item "Teobalt's stapler" -1
        @flag stapler_returned
        @coins 10
        @aff teobalt +4 | Returned his stapler
      > "Would you play a game with me at some point?"
        [teobalt] "Thursday. I am making a list."
        A faint, regretful smile.
        [teobalt] "It is an excellent offer. I accept it for Thursday."
        @flag teobalt_thursday
      > "Sorry to bother you."
        [teobalt] "It is no bother. It is, at worst, an interruption. And I like those, in moderation."
    @time 6
  @else
    [teobalt] "Rowan. Is the list working?"
    @if has_unloading_list
      [rowan] "It's wonderful. It's teal."
      [teobalt] "Teal. Yes." A very small, very pleased pause. "Nobody ever notices the teal."
      @aff teobalt +2 | Noticed the teal
    @time 3
    @if found_stapler and not stapler_returned
      @choice
        > Hand him the stapler you found.
          [teobalt] "That is — " He stops. He takes it in both hands, turns it over, finds his own stencil, and for a moment says nothing at all. "Item two. Solved."
          [teobalt] "I had it down as stolen. I had made a list of suspects." A very small, very bright pause. "You are not on it. You have never been on it."
          [teobalt] "Item three, since you have earned it, is the tuba. Somebody boarded with a tuba, and nobody has claimed it. A thing nobody owns is a thing nobody will collect."
          @item "Teobalt's stapler" -1
          @flag stapler_returned
          @flag teobalt_tuba_quest
          @coins 10
          @aff teobalt +4 | Returned his stapler
        > Keep it a little longer, as a joke.
          [teobalt] "…I beg your pardon?"
          [rowan] "Never mind. Here."
    @if not chat_teobalt_done
      @choice
        > "Have you got a moment between items? I've got a few questions."
          @scene chat_teobalt
        > "I'll let you get back to it."
  @if met:teobalt and not chat_teobalt_offered
    @flag chat_teobalt_offered
    @scene chat_teobalt
